A recurrence relation is a mathematical equation that describes a sequence of values where each value is defined as a function of the previous values. In the context of algorithmic scrutiny, recurrence relations are used to describe the time complexity of algorithms, particularly those that use recursion.

The time complexity of an algorithm is a measure of how the running time of the algorithm grows as the size of the input increases. This is often expressed in Big O notation, which describes the upper bound of the growth rate of the running time.

In the case of recursive algorithms, the time complexity can often be described using a recurrence relation. For example, consider the recursive algorithm for finding the factorial of a number:

```
function factorial(n) {
  if (n == 0) {
    return 1;
  } else {
    return n * factorial(n - 1);
  }
}
```

The time complexity of this algorithm can be described by the recurrence relation T(n) = T(n - 1) + O(1), where T(n) is the time complexity of the algorithm for an input of size n, and O(1) represents a constant time operation.

Solving this recurrence relation gives us the time complexity of the algorithm, which in this case is O(n), indicating that the running time of the algorithm grows linearly with the size of the input.

In conclusion, recurrence relations are a powerful tool for analyzing the time complexity of recursive algorithms, and are an essential part of algorithmic scrutiny.

def merge_sort_descending(arr):
    if len(arr) <= 1:
        return arr
    mid = len(arr) // 2
    left_half = merge_sort_descending(arr[:mid])
    right_half = merge_sort_descending(arr[mid:])
    return merge_descending(left_half, right_half)

def merge_descending(left, right):
    sorted_list = []
    i = j = 0
    while i < len(left) and j < len(right):
        if left[i] > right[j]:
            sorted_list.append(left[i])
            i += 1
        else:
            sorted_list.append(right[j])
            j += 1
    sorted_list.extend(left[i:])
    sorted_list.extend(right[j:])
    return sorted_list

